---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const title = "How to Sign Thank You, Sorry and Please (ASL & BSL)";
const description =
  "The three politeness signs every learner needs in both ASL and BSL — step-by-step instructions, the shared BSL please/thank-you form, and when to use each.";
const datePublished = "2026-10-17";
const slug = "how-to-sign-thank-you-sorry-please";
const pageUrl = `https://signlanguage-translator.com/learn/${slug}/`;

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Are PLEASE and SORRY the same sign in ASL?",
    a: "No — and this is the classic beginner mix-up. ASL PLEASE uses a flat open hand circled on the chest; ASL SORRY uses a loose fist circled on the chest. Same location, same circular motion — the handshape is the whole difference. Learn them as a pair so you never confuse an apology with a request.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do you sign 'you're welcome' in ASL?",
    a: "'You're welcome' is its own separate sign — not a variation of PLEASE or THANK YOU. This guide covers thanks, apologies, and requests only; look up 'you're welcome' as a standalone entry in the dictionary and learn it the same step-by-step way.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do you sign 'thank you very much'?",
    a: "Intensify rather than invent: sign THANK YOU with a slightly larger, more deliberate movement and a warm, sincere expression. In general, sign languages mark degree through movement size and facial intensity — you don't need a different sign for 'very much'.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do you respond when someone thanks you in sign language?",
    a: "Acknowledge it warmly — a nod, a smile, and the sign for 'you're welcome' in whichever language you're using. Culturally, brushing off thanks the way some spoken conversations do ('oh, it was nothing') isn't the norm; accept the thanks graciously.",
  },
  {
    q: "Why do PLEASE and THANK YOU look the same in BSL?",
    a: "In BSL they share one manual form — fingertips to the chin, then the hand moves away from the face. The meaning comes from mouthing: silently mouth 'please' or 'thank you' as you sign. This is normal BSL grammar, not sloppiness; the mouth pattern is part of the sign.",
  },
  {
    q: "Should I sign SORRY when I bump into someone?",
    a: "For a light bump, most signers use EXCUSE ME instead — it carries 'oops, my fault, passing through' better. Reserve SORRY for genuine apologies: mistakes, misunderstandings, lateness. Using the apology sign for every minor bump dilutes it.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can I use the ASL signs with BSL signers and vice versa?",
    a: "No. ASL and BSL are separate languages with different vocabularies — a BSL signer will not recognise the ASL signs above, and the reverse. Learn the set for the language you are studying; this guide covers both so you can compare them side by side.",
  },
];

const articleJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  headline: title,
  description,
  datePublished,
  author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Firoz Khan" },
  publisher: {
    "@type": "Organization",
    name: "SignLanguage-Translator.com",
  },
  mainEntityOfPage: pageUrl,
};

const faqJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: f.a },
  })),
};
---

<BaseLayout title={`${title} | Sign Language Translator`} description={description}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(articleJsonLd)} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqJsonLd)} />

  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-14 sm:px-6">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <ol class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 text-sm text-slate-500">
        <li><a class="underline-offset-2 hover:underline" href="/">Home</a></li>
        <li aria-hidden="true">/</li>
        <li><a class="underline-offset-2 hover:underline" href="/learn/">Learn</a></li>
        <li aria-hidden="true">/</li>
        <li aria-current="page" class="font-semibold text-slate-900">{title}</li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <h1 class="mt-6 text-4xl font-extrabold text-slate-900">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-500">
      <time datetime={datePublished}>{datePublished}</time> · By Firoz Khan · 6 min read
    </p>

    <article class="prose-lg mt-6 space-y-5 text-slate-700">
      <p>
        The short answer: ASL signs THANK YOU from the chin outward, PLEASE
        with a flat hand circling the chest, and SORRY with a fist circling
        the chest. In BSL, THANK YOU and PLEASE share one manual form —
        fingertips to the chin, hand moving away — and mouthing tells them
        apart ("please" vs "thank you"), while SORRY is a fist circling the
        chest with "sorry" mouthed.
      </p>
      <p>
        ASL and BSL are separate languages, so learn the set for the one
        you're studying. Below are step-by-step instructions for all six
        signs, plus when to use each word and the mix-ups to avoid.
      </p>
      <figure class="my-6 text-center">
        <div class="flex items-end justify-center gap-8">
          <div>
            <img class="mx-auto h-40 w-auto" loading="lazy" src="/alphabet/t.svg" alt="ASL handshape for the letter T — the initial of thank-you" />
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-slate-700">ASL T</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <img class="mx-auto h-40 w-auto" loading="lazy" src="/bsl-alphabet/t.svg" alt="BSL handshape for the letter T — the initial of thank-you" />
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-slate-700">BSL T</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <img class="mx-auto h-40 w-auto" loading="lazy" src="/alphabet/p.svg" alt="ASL handshape for the letter P — the initial of please" />
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-slate-700">ASL P</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <img class="mx-auto h-40 w-auto" loading="lazy" src="/bsl-alphabet/p.svg" alt="BSL handshape for the letter P — the initial of please" />
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-slate-700">BSL P</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figcaption class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-500">
          The initials of thank-you and please in the two different alphabets — a reminder that ASL and BSL share no handshapes.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">ASL: Thank you</h2>
      <p>
        Touch the fingertips of your flat dominant hand to your chin,
        palm facing toward you. Then move the hand forward and slightly
        downward, ending with the palm angled upward — as if sending
        gratitude forward to the other person. One smooth motion; no
        need to repeat it. See the full reference on our{" "}
        <a class="font-semibold text-indigo-700 underline" href="/signs/thank-you/">THANK YOU sign page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">ASL: Please</h2>
      <p>
        Place your flat dominant hand on your chest, palm down, and rub
        it in a small clockwise circle. Picture a flat hand circling the
        heart. Note the contrast with SORRY below: PLEASE is a{" "}
        <strong>flat hand</strong>, SORRY is a <strong>fist</strong> —
        same location, same circular motion, different handshape. Mixing
        these up is the classic beginner error, so learn them as a pair.{" "}
        <a class="font-semibold text-indigo-700 underline" href="/signs/please/">PLEASE sign page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">ASL: Sorry</h2>
      <p>
        Make a loose fist with your palm facing your chest, and rub it
        in small circles over your heart. Hold the apologetic intent —
        this sign reads as a genuine apology, so pair it with a sincere
        expression rather than a casual brush-off. For light, incidental
        bumps, signers often use EXCUSE ME instead.{" "}
        <a class="font-semibold text-indigo-700 underline" href="/signs/sorry/">SORRY sign page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">BSL: Thank you</h2>
      <p>
        Touch the fingertips of your flat dominant hand to your chin,
        then move the hand down and away from your face in one smooth
        motion. Mouth the words "thank you" silently as you sign — in
        BSL, mouthing is part of many signs and helps make meaning
        unmistakable. A small, quick movement is all you need in everyday
        conversation.{" "}
        <a class="font-semibold text-indigo-700 underline" href="/bsl-signs/thank-you/">THANK YOU sign page (BSL)</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">BSL: Please — the same sign, mouthing decides</h2>
      <p>
        Here is the twist. Touch the fingertips of your flat dominant
        hand to your chin and move the hand slightly forward and away
        from your face — exactly the same manual form as THANK YOU. What
        distinguishes the two is the mouth: silently mouth "please"
        while you sign. This is not a shortcut or a mistake; in BSL the
        mouth pattern carries meaning and is a legitimate part of the
        sign. Practise both with a mirror and watch yourself switch
        meaning with your mouth alone.{" "}
        <a class="font-semibold text-indigo-700 underline" href="/bsl-signs/please/">PLEASE sign page (BSL)</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">BSL: Sorry</h2>
      <p>
        Close your dominant hand into a fist, hold it against the centre
        of your chest, and move it in small circles against your chest.
        Mouth "sorry" silently while signing to make it unmistakable.{" "}
        <a class="font-semibold text-indigo-700 underline" href="/bsl-signs/sorry/">SORRY sign page (BSL)</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">ASL vs BSL: the three signs side by side</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left text-slate-700">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b-2 border-slate-300">
              <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-bold text-slate-900">Sign</th>
              <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-bold text-slate-900">ASL</th>
              <th class="py-2 font-bold text-slate-900">BSL</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="border-b border-slate-200">
              <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">THANK YOU</td>
              <td class="py-2 pr-4">Fingertips to chin, hand moves forward and down</td>
              <td class="py-2">Fingertips to chin, hand moves down and away — mouthing "thank you"</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-slate-200">
              <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">PLEASE</td>
              <td class="py-2 pr-4">Flat hand circling the chest</td>
              <td class="py-2">Same manual form as BSL THANK YOU — mouthing "please" decides the meaning</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">SORRY</td>
              <td class="py-2 pr-4">Fist circling the chest</td>
              <td class="py-2">Fist circling the chest — mouthing "sorry"</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">When to use each one</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
        <li>
          <strong>Thank you:</strong> the universal gratitude sign — for
          help, gifts, compliments, or when someone holds a door. Works
          anywhere, in both languages.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Please:</strong> softens requests — "please help me,"
          "please repeat that." Use it the way you would in speech: in
          requests, not in every sentence.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Sorry:</strong> genuine apologies — mistakes,
          misunderstandings, lateness. Don't dilute it by using it for
          every minor bump; EXCUSE ME exists for those.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Across languages:</strong> learn the set for the
          language you're studying. An ASL sign won't be recognised by a
          BSL signer, and the BSL mouthing system won't carry meaning
          for an ASL signer.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Common mistakes to avoid</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
        <li>
          <strong>Mixing up ASL PLEASE and SORRY.</strong> Same spot, same
          circular motion — the only difference is flat hand (PLEASE) vs
          fist (SORRY). Drill them as a pair until the handshape is
          automatic.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Signing BSL PLEASE/THANK YOU without mouthing.</strong>
          The manual form is identical, so skipping the mouth pattern
          removes the only thing telling them apart. Mouth the word every
          time.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Using SORRY for every bump.</strong> A genuine apology
          sign for a light accidental bump is overkill — EXCUSE ME is the
          right tool for "oops, passing through."
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Smiling through SORRY.</strong> Your face should match
          the sign's intent. A grin undercuts the apology and can read as
          insincere.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Using ASL signs with BSL signers (or vice versa).</strong>
          These are separate languages. A BSL signer won't recognise the
          ASL PLEASE, and the BSL mouthing system won't carry meaning for
          an ASL signer.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <dl class="space-y-4">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5">
            <dt class="font-bold text-slate-900">{f.q}</dt>
            <dd class="mt-2 text-slate-700">{f.a}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Keep learning</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
        <li>
          <a class="font-semibold text-indigo-700 underline" href="/learn/common-asl-greetings/">Common ASL greetings</a>{" "}
          — hello, goodbye, and morning-to-night greetings to open conversations.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a class="font-semibold text-indigo-700 underline" href="/learn/essential-bsl-signs-for-beginners/">Essential BSL signs for beginners</a>{" "}
          — the core BSL vocabulary to build on after politeness.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a class="font-semibold text-indigo-700 underline" href="/learn/asl-vs-bsl-key-differences/">ASL vs BSL: key differences</a>{" "}
          — why the two languages share almost no vocabulary.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <div
      role="note"
      class="mt-10 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 px-5 py-4 text-sm text-slate-700"
    >
      <p>
        A learning aid, not a certified interpreter. Sign guides are a
        starting point — real fluency comes from learning with Deaf instructors
        and the Deaf community.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
